organization at Google.

00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:25.350
But I don't actually work
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.930
Instead, my team and I have
spent the past few years

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.850
building an enterprise
application exactly

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.030
as if we were an
external team using just

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.209
the publicly available
features, services,

00:00:35.209 --> 00:00:36.875
and everything of
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.160
So if you're going to build a
wildly successful application,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.390
first you have to
decide what to build.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.840
Cloud Platform's not going
to really help you here.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.760
For us, we actually
almost stumbled

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.130
upon this is that we were
trying to grow our team.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.426
We had to hire people.

00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:54.800
And everyone we
talked to kind of

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.633
had horror stories about
how that had worked

00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:58.072
in the past at other companies.

00:00:58.072 --> 00:00:59.030
It was really everyone.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.460
It was from the candidates
to the recruiters

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.090
to the employees at the
interview to hiring managers.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.340
And so we said, let's build
this little prototype just

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.090
to manage this process
and make it better.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.381
We won't spend much time on it.

00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:11.660
It's not that interesting.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.590
So we built the prototype,
started using it.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.560
And the more we dug into
that space, realized,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.900
oh, this is kind of interesting.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.000
Like, hiring is something
every company has to do.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.959
It's a critical
business process.

00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:24.500
And so from that we
said, well, let's

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.110
actually make this
prototype our product.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.580
And we dove right in.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.050
So for you, choose something.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.810
Please don't choose hiring.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:33.590
What did we build?

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.170
We called it Hire--

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.324
super descriptive.

00:01:37.324 --> 00:01:38.240
And what does Hire do?

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.690
It manages the entire process
of finding new employees

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:47.420
from sourcing to job postings to
scheduling interviews to adding

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.350
feedback to kind of
basically hiring the person.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.209
And we integrate
with all of the tools

00:01:53.209 --> 00:01:54.750
in G Suite you
already know and love,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.170
like Gmail, Calendar, Sheets,
Drive, Docs, et cetera.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.730
And actually, even
now we're starting

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.770
to add some more AI
powered features, which

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.210
I'll talk a bit more later.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.970
And are we wildly successful?

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.230
I'm maybe not the best
person to judge this,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.400
but we launched
roughly a year ago.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.970
And since then, at least
according to some industry

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.760
press, we're the number one
growing app in the space.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.560
And beyond just
customer growth, we've

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.893
got a wide breadth of customers
in different industries,

00:02:22.893 --> 00:02:26.800
from retail to health to
restaurant to construction.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.370
And I guess a little
bit in tech, as well.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.190
Beyond just that, also, is
our customers seem to like us.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:34.750
We run these little
surveys in app

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.520
which we called happiness
tracking surveys, which

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.061
are basically customers give us
a thumbs up or a thumbs down.

00:02:39.061 --> 00:02:41.800
And we get mostly thumbs up.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.620
So we've built a wildly
successful application.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.446
How did Cloud
platform get us there?

00:02:47.446 --> 00:02:49.570
So normally when you're
choosing what cloud to use,

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.670
it's between Google
and I think there's

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.340
two other big names that
come up which I can't quite

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.800
remember right now.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.810
But for us, we're
actually a team at Google.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.790
And Google already has
the best internal cloud

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.880
in the world for building,
deploying, and maintaining

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.350
global scale services.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.410
I guess I'm not biased there.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.884
So why aren't we using the
internal Cloud at Google?

00:03:10.884 --> 00:03:12.050
There's a number of reasons.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.550
First off, the internal Cloud
is notoriously difficult to use.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.370
People talk about if you have
a new employee that comes on,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.230
it's going to take them six
months before they're useful.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.960
And beyond that, also
people joke about

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.335
that once you've
actually come up

00:03:24.335 --> 00:03:26.164
to speed, if you
want to use anything,

00:03:26.164 --> 00:03:27.580
all the other
services internally,

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.100
they're either currently in
beta or already deprecated.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.600
So if you're building
something, you

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.630
are forced to choose between
something that's beta and buggy

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.060
or something that's
deprecated and you're

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.560
going to have to
switch to something

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.810
new in a little while, anyways.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.650
Cloud Platform,
on the other hand,

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.210
is stable, documented, public.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.874
You can actually hire
people with experience.

00:03:48.874 --> 00:03:51.040
It's got a coherent user
interface, coherent command

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.454
line tools, all sorts of
things to recommend it.

00:03:54.454 --> 00:03:55.870
None of those are
the real reason.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.020
The real reason-- it's dog food.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.540
Mmm, dog food.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.980
OK, people don't say that.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.355
But here at Google,
we talk a lot

00:04:04.355 --> 00:04:06.830
about dogfooding and
eating your own dog food.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.740
This is actually an
industry term, I believe.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.290
You can look up
the Wikipedia page.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.370
But what this means is you
basically volunteer or force

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.230
your employees to use the
software you're building.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.019
And as the name implies,
this isn't always

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:23.480
that pleasant of an experience,
even here in San Francisco,

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.330
where the dog food's free-range,
organic, asbestos-free.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.440
So what we did is for Hire
is that we built Hire.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.231
We've been using Hire
since, basically, day

00:04:32.231 --> 00:04:33.509
zero to hire everyone.

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:35.050
And that means our
first set of users

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.360
are people right down the hall
where they can go yell at you

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:38.840
and say, this
feature doesn't work.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.330
There's a bug.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.041
I don't understand
why you did this.

00:04:42.041 --> 00:04:44.290
And you could use that
feedback to make things better.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.330
And I guess very
seldomly they'll tell you

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.540
this feature is awesome.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.170
It doesn't happen much.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.570
More than this, though, is
that our entire product,

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.506
our entire Hire product,
is we're building it

00:04:54.506 --> 00:04:55.380
as if we're external.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.120
So we're actually doing a dog
food of all of Google Cloud

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:58.660
platform.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.970
We're building an application
just like you guys

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.690
are building applications.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.830
And then we can provide
hopefully trusted,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.050
coherent feedback to
the Cloud Platform

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.480
team about what works, what
doesn't work, et cetera.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.199
And this dogfooding process
is really, really important.

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:14.740
And if you're building
an application

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.450
that you're not
dogfooding, think

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:20.020
about building something else.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.020
Yeah, so, eat your own dog food.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.245
It's good for you.

00:05:23.245 --> 00:05:26.450
And now on to how we
built the dog food.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.620
What did we use in
Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.510
A little bit first
about Hire architecture.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.020
We're actually a really pretty
standard enterprise app.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.600
We're build on something
I call the SEAN stack.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.751
I'm pretty sure no one else
in the world uses that.

00:05:39.751 --> 00:05:41.250
It's basically the
MEAN stack, which

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.460
is Mongo, Express, Angular,
JS, NodeJS, where we've swapped

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.580
out Mongo with Cloud Spanner.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.627
You can see the diagram
here is pretty standard.

00:05:49.627 --> 00:05:50.460
We have web browser.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.750
We're using AngularJS,
a mixture of JavaScript,

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.210
and we're now moving to
TypeScript there, packaged up

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.480
everything there with
Webpack, which we love.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.806
And we're talking to various
NodeJS app server back ends.

00:06:03.806 --> 00:06:06.180
Those back ends spend all
their time either writing stuff

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.820
to Spanner or Cloud storage
or making, basically,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.890
API calls to the public Gmail,
Docs, Calendars, et cetera.

00:06:16.711 --> 00:06:18.210
This is a place
like source control.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.990
You have to pick something here.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.110
Google actually has this
amazing internal system.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.234
We've written papers on it.

00:06:23.234 --> 00:06:24.330
It's called Piper.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.180
It does the whole source
control, code review,

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.470
I guess repository
management and tests as well.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.720
Unfortunately, we're acting
as if we're a third party,

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.220
so we can't use any of that.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.040
Instead, we chose git,
everyone's favorite source

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.021
control system.

00:06:39.021 --> 00:06:41.520
My thing that I like about Git
is that if you can figure out

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.960
how to use Git,
it makes actually

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.570
writing your application
seem easy in comparison.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.330
You think I'm
joking, but I'm not.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.540
It's my favorite Git man page.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.492
As far as for our repository
management and code review

00:06:57.492 --> 00:06:58.950
tool, we use
something like Gerrit.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:02.924
I believe that was spun out
of Google many years ago.

00:07:02.924 --> 00:07:05.340
For a while, I used to complain
how much worse than GitHub

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:05.840
it was.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.140
I actually kind of like it now.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.389
It's under active development.

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:09.110
It's open source.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:10.440
It's rapidly getting better.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.334
And then beyond just
this, even though we're

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.750
using different
tools, we're still

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.720
following the standard
Google code review policies.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.430
Every change gets code reviewed.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:24.000
Everything has tests.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.895
Speaking of testing, I said
we have a homegrown test

00:07:28.895 --> 00:07:29.520
infrastructure.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.910
Totally don't do this.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:32.970
Takes lots of time.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.500
And when we were
first starting out,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.380
we evaluated everything out
there with third parties.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.850
And at that time, Cloud
Platform was relatively new.

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:41.266
There was nothing
we really liked.

00:07:41.266 --> 00:07:43.710
So we went off
and built our own.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.680
Today I think the Cloud
Build was announced.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:47.850
Definitely look at that.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.350
And I believe all
the third parties,

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:52.350
at this point,
support Google Cloud.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.650
But for us, what we have, we're
actually pretty happy in it.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.410
Any time you push a code
review, it gets tested

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:01.170
and you get your results
back in 30 minutes or less.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:04.490
We run basically our entire
test suite, which is, right now,

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:10.680
I think 1,500 tests or 1,500
suites, 20,000 test cases.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.760
And I really test this.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.040
Like, don't tell anyone, but
I often make code changes

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.670
without ever
running them locally

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.310
because if our tests
pass, it's good.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.660
And that pretty much works out.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.310
And yeah, again, don't do this.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:23.110
Use a third party.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:25.320
But if you do this,
there's lots of things

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:26.850
here that Cloud
Platform has helped

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.808
us is that all of our
artifacts for all of this

00:08:28.808 --> 00:08:30.310
are stored in Cloud storage.

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:34.659
So this is like the test runner
logs, the app server logs.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.539
For our end-to-end
browser tests,

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:38.700
we actually record
videos of where we click.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.825
And so if you have a front
end test that's failing,

00:08:40.825 --> 00:08:43.870
you can just click that video
and watch what's happening.

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.510
The other piece we use a lot
here is we're using BigQuery.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.760
I'll mention that a lot today.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.260
We really love BigQuery.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.840
And so all the test runs,
the results goes in there.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.860
We can figure out
which tests are flaky,

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.740
automatically rerun them.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.540
And then also for
scheduling we want

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.080
to run our longest tests first.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.189
And so that's also in BigQuery.

00:09:01.189 --> 00:09:03.230
The other thing we like
is we use preemptible VMs

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.840
to keep costs down.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.970
And here's a quote from,
basically, the engineer

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:08.930
that built all of this.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:11.180
And his quote was, "Don't
use preemptible VMs."

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:12.680
And really what he
meant was that we

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.805
had a system that was built
without preemptive VMs.

00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:18.470
And when they came out, we said,
oh, let's just switch to those.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.870
And retrofitting it in
turned out to be really hard.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:24.470
That the number of new
failure modes added,

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:26.720
it just took us a long time
to get back to stability.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.761
But definitely if you're
starting from beginning,

00:09:28.761 --> 00:09:31.110
definitely use preemptible VMs.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.260
Other thing that's maybe
a theme here is definitely

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:34.436
read the documentation.

00:09:34.436 --> 00:09:35.810
The exact quote
from the docs are

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:39.110
preemptible instances are
excess compute engine capacity,

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.180
so their availability
varies with usage,

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:42.555
which doesn't mean
that if you're

00:09:42.555 --> 00:09:45.590
using only preemptible VMs,
any point they may all go away.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:46.715
That did happen to us once.

00:09:46.715 --> 00:09:47.990
It hasn't happened recently.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:49.610
But just something
to keep in mind.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:50.770
Luckily, it's really easy.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:54.620
It's a single flag to
switch back to regular VMs.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.590
So we've talked about testing.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.841
How do we actually
deploy things?

00:09:58.841 --> 00:10:01.090
I actually tried to draw a
picture of what our release

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.020
process looked like.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:04.090
And it started looking
like one of those diagrams

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.256
from a serial killer movie
where you've got pictures

00:10:06.256 --> 00:10:07.900
and string and all of that.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.085
So instead, I just put that
picture of the canary here.

00:10:11.085 --> 00:10:12.960
So I'm going to try to
explain this verbally.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.940
Basically, we do push
code daily to prod.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.582
But we only release features
to our customers monthly.

00:10:19.582 --> 00:10:21.040
What this means is
all feature work

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.150
is hidden behind
essentially runtime flags

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.884
as we've got a table
in our database

00:10:24.884 --> 00:10:26.800
that lists whether or
not a feature is enabled

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.740
for a certain individual user,
a customer, or for everyone.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.220
And this breaking up your
code pushes from your releases

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.250
makes things so much simpler.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.320
And how do our
code releases work?

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.810
Or how our code pushes work
is basically I make a change.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.180
It gets pushed up to Gerrit.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.860
Our tests automatically
run on it.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:48.570
Say thumbs up or thumbs down.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:49.740
Colleagues do code reviews.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:50.810
Thumbs up or thumbs down.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:54.010
It's usually a few thumbs
down until thumbs up.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:55.260
At that point, we check it in.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.509
Next day it gets deployed
to our staging system, which

00:10:57.509 --> 00:10:59.820
is basically an exact
copy of production

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.400
with some minor configuration
changes like different crypto

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.240
keys and that kind of thing.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.250
And then we do
manual testing there.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.014
And then the next
day, it gets pushed

00:11:08.014 --> 00:11:09.930
to what we call our
canaries to the customers,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.689
thus the picture.

00:11:11.689 --> 00:11:13.230
And those are
basically the customers

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.396
we randomly selected that
get to try out new things.

00:11:15.396 --> 00:11:18.750
And if there's any problems
there, we stop and roll back.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.959
We do the same thing for
our monthly feature releases

00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:22.416
is that some features
are obviously

00:11:22.416 --> 00:11:24.030
in development for many months.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:28.500
And so we actually have a longer
roll out process for those.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.930
We first roll out to our
dog food tenants, which

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.300
is basically our use of
Hire, maybe our sales

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.920
demos and a couple other things.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.049
And next, if it's
a big feature, we

00:11:37.049 --> 00:11:39.090
might roll out to what we
call our trusted tester

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.700
customers, which is a set of
customers that have agreed

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:42.632
to have beta features.

00:11:42.632 --> 00:11:43.590
And we'll get feedback.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.300
And it may stay
there for months.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.425
And after that, it kind of
follows the same process

00:11:47.425 --> 00:11:48.360
as our code pushes.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.030
It goes to the canary and
then usually the next day

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:54.290
to all customers.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.877
And all these different--
like, we have this production.

00:11:57.877 --> 00:11:58.710
I mentioned staging.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:11:59.990
We also have performance
environments,

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:01.448
where a set of
environments that we

00:12:01.448 --> 00:12:03.320
have simulated really
large customers

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:05.180
and are running
performance tests.

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.340
All of these different
configurations

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:09.290
are almost the
same but different.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:13.170
And this is one place where our
Cloud Platform really helps us.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:17.130
We have all of our config is
checked into our code system.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:18.830
And I'll talk more
about that later.

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:21.290
It's basically our
release process.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:24.460
So what do we use
in Cloud Platform

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.270
to build our wildly
successful application?

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.824
And what do they help?

00:12:28.824 --> 00:12:30.240
There's lots of
things we've used.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:33.920
I'm just going to cover
the big ones here.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:34.700
All right.

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.050
This is kind of a tired analogy.

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.259
It's the cattle versus pets.

00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:38.675
And I think people
like me include

00:12:38.675 --> 00:12:39.883
it here just to give a break.

00:12:39.883 --> 00:12:43.560
And we can all admire
the cute animals.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.110
But what this means, if
you're not familiar with this,

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.202
is in the bad old days, we used
to treat servers like pets.

00:12:49.202 --> 00:12:50.160
You'd give them a name.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.840
You'd hug them
and care for them.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.390
And if one of them got sick,
you would take it to the vet

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.110
and make it better.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.524
Nowadays is, yeah,
don't do that.

00:12:57.524 --> 00:12:59.190
Treat your servers
as if they're cattle.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.455
They're interchangeable.

00:13:01.455 --> 00:13:02.610
And how do you do this?

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.950
This is where Kubernetes
Engine-- we're basically all

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.235
on Kubernetes at this point.

00:13:07.235 --> 00:13:08.610
And what's nice
about that is you

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.512
can have all of your
configuration checked in.

00:13:11.512 --> 00:13:13.470
And if it's checked in,
you can do code reviews

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.140
on the configuration
and then run tests.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.250
And we do that.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:18.750
And I'll talk about
this later more,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.916
but like configuration
changes are really dangerous,

00:13:20.916 --> 00:13:22.099
as far as running a service.

00:13:22.099 --> 00:13:23.640
And another nice
thing, if you're not

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.556
familiar with Kubernetes,
is the configuration

00:13:25.556 --> 00:13:27.570
isn't like a set of
steps, things to deploy.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.966
It's a description of what your
environment should look like.

00:13:30.966 --> 00:13:32.340
And so you can
start doing things

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.170
like self-healing
on this, diffing it

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.380
when you make a change, all
sorts of really nice things.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:39.985
Took us kind of quite
a while to get there.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:41.610
I think our first
version here was just

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.140
a set of shell scripts.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.640
We were making Cloud API calls.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:49.440
And then we said, OK, let's
use Deployment Manager configs.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.000
We had those checked
in at that point

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:54.580
and we were using Google
Container Engine for that--

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:56.040
Compute Engine for that, sorry.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:58.020
And this all worked fine.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.750
We were kind of
unhappy with this.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.047
The resource
utilization was bad.

00:14:03.047 --> 00:14:05.130
Things like rolling updates
we were having trouble

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.392
getting them to be as
fast as we wanted to.

00:14:07.392 --> 00:14:09.350
And our configuration
was starting to get messy

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:13.020
and so we wrote a templating
tool for that in Python

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:13.600
this time.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.558
And that helped a little
bit, but we were still

00:14:15.558 --> 00:14:17.590
making mistakes that we
didn't think we should.

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.010
And so about that
time, Kubernetes

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:21.870
came out and was in production.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:24.276
So we switched over to that.

00:14:24.276 --> 00:14:25.170
And this was great.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:27.810
We got all the benefits as
our rolling updates were fast.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.296
Our system was more stable.

00:14:30.296 --> 00:14:32.280
Our config files were
still kind of-- we

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.040
had checked in YAML files and
they were still kind of big.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.540
And so we were
having problems where

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:38.370
people would update
a staging environment

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:40.575
and then forget to
update the production.

00:14:40.575 --> 00:14:42.087
So it was error prone.

00:14:42.087 --> 00:14:43.920
We wrote, actually, a
templating tool there.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.070
We used Go, I guess because you
have to use all the languages.

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:52.027
So it's still a problem.

00:14:52.027 --> 00:14:54.360
And so this year we switched
to something called jsonit.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.890
This is another
open source project

00:14:55.890 --> 00:15:00.079
inspired by features of
Google internal cloud.

00:15:00.079 --> 00:15:02.370
We haven't been using this
for long enough, but so far,

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:02.910
so happy.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:05.810
Our configuration
files are smaller.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:07.190
Still checked in.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:08.880
And we're using,
basically, this for all

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.200
of our Kubernetes config.

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:13.770
We have a little bit of
Deployment Manager still.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.510
As far as what in
Kubernetes do we use,

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:19.550
we have a pretty
straightforward setup here.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:21.860
We have, I think, one
cluster, a couple of node,

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:28.700
pools a handful of services,
so not terribly interesting.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:32.330
We love the rolling updates
we get for new changes.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.060
I was talking to people, we're
actually not really using

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.500
auto scaling very much.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.360
That's probably
because we're at Google

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:40.070
and we never actually
get the bills.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.861
I know that's on our
list of things to fix.

00:15:41.861 --> 00:15:45.449
But I was a little surprised
to figure that out.

00:15:45.449 --> 00:15:47.240
Yes, as I said, we're
mostly on Kubernetes.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.250
We have a couple of Cloud
Functions we're using.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:52.130
They're super easy to spin up.

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:54.115
I'm a little anxious of
these because maybe one

00:15:54.115 --> 00:15:57.470
of the first ones I used is I
didn't read the documentation.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:58.690
See that theme?

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:00.530
And there's a 10
megabyte serving limit.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:04.100
And so our customers grew
and we were merging XML files

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:04.950
for a job feed.

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.560
And that went past 10 megabytes.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.569
And we had outage.

00:16:07.569 --> 00:16:09.110
So I'm still a little
scared of them.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.235
And maybe that's just,
we're not using them enough.

00:16:11.235 --> 00:16:14.780
But just something
to think there.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:15.719
All right, storage.

00:16:15.719 --> 00:16:17.260
I said Cloud Spanner,
basically we're

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.360
using this for all of
our relational data.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:21.610
And I'm kind of a
little bit fuzzy here.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.350
We actually predate
use of Cloud Spanner.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.599
So we have something that's
almost like Cloud Spanner,

00:16:25.599 --> 00:16:26.670
but not.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:28.130
But we love it.

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:30.130
We have no downtime
for our schema changes,

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:33.460
really no downtime for anything.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:35.750
Some lessons learned
here, I guess,

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.500
talking about schema changes
is there's still a pain,

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:39.844
as far as for an app writer.

00:16:39.844 --> 00:16:41.260
It's still our
biggest pain point.

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:44.269
We haven't found a
magic solution for that.

00:16:44.269 --> 00:16:46.810
Other kind of thing is if you
look at the different modes you

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:49.390
can set up Spanner
in this, I would

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:51.280
say go to multi-regional first.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:54.580
If your thinking is that we
had to re-architect things

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.130
in our app a little bit when
we wanted to go multi-regional.

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:59.510
Multi-regional actually
slows down your commits.

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:01.635
And for various
reasons, we have an app

00:17:01.635 --> 00:17:03.760
what we call little green
dots everywhere that show

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.093
whether or not you've read
something like a new comment.

00:17:06.093 --> 00:17:08.685
And what that meant
is that as you're

00:17:08.685 --> 00:17:10.060
navigating around
our app, things

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.976
that should be read-only
views actually become

00:17:11.976 --> 00:17:13.119
writes to Spanner.

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:15.079
And we were doing those in-line.

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.005
And so when we wanted
to do it multi-regional,

00:17:17.005 --> 00:17:18.880
the transactions for
those became slow enough

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.755
we had to do a little
bit of re-architecting.

00:17:20.755 --> 00:17:24.339
And your app writers get unhappy
when your database people

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:25.780
make everything slower for you.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:29.810
So I would say,
write that off first.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:30.410
What else?

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:31.074
Cloud storage.

00:17:31.074 --> 00:17:33.740
We're using that lots of places,
as I mentioned, testing already

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:34.810
in app.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:36.410
We use this for all
the resumes people

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:40.077
upload, company
logos, cover letters.

00:17:40.077 --> 00:17:42.410
And we actually use it for
all of the static assets that

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:47.480
go as part of our web page, like
the CSS files, the HTML files.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:48.170
We love it.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.300
No real problems here.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:53.630
Maybe one lessons
learned here is

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.320
I was looking at the load time
of our app versus the load

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.920
time of the main Google page.

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:00.050
Why are my CSS files
taking so much longer

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:01.900
than google.com's CSS files?

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:07.670
And it turns out I'd enabled
the multi-regional cloud storage

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:11.261
but had not turned on the
Cloud CDN in front of it.

00:18:11.261 --> 00:18:13.010
And so the multi-regional,
if you read it,

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.580
it kind of seems like
that's all you need.

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.370
But if you're actually
serving up assets,

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:19.370
you also need to enable
Cloud CDN, as well.

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:23.050
That's an easy change, though.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:25.104
Memory store, this is,
at least the time I

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:26.770
wrote this presentation,
it was in beta.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:28.561
I think lots of things
are being announced.

00:18:28.561 --> 00:18:30.556
So I'm not sure if that's
changed today or not.

00:18:30.556 --> 00:18:31.930
We're really
interested in moving

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.135
some of our internal
caching over to this.

00:18:34.135 --> 00:18:36.010
Lessons learned here is
that we're definitely

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:38.980
not switching over to it
until it's out of beta.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:43.090
There's a reason services
are in beta, so believe that.

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.920
Network-- notice the nice slide.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:46.420
Colleague and I
were actually trying

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:49.120
to talk and figure out what
should we put on this slide?

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:51.444
And his quote was, oh, the
nice thing about this is,

00:18:51.444 --> 00:18:53.110
or the praise here
is, we don't actually

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:54.401
have to talk about this at all.

00:18:54.401 --> 00:18:55.280
It just works.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.045
So we have a relatively
simple networking.

00:18:57.045 --> 00:18:58.420
We're using the
Google Cloud load

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.956
balancers, SSL termination.

00:19:00.956 --> 00:19:02.830
We're actually still
single region right now,

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:04.070
so that makes things
a bit simpler.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:06.430
We've prototyped what we
need to do for multi-regional

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:08.570
and it doesn't really
change anything.

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.930
So network, we're really happy.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:12.970
If I have to complain
about anything,

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:15.610
I would complain a tiny
bit about cloud CDN,

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.869
as I wished it had a
better invalidation API.

00:19:18.869 --> 00:19:21.160
You can invalidate things in
it, but you can't actually

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:22.600
make it part of your
normal workflow.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.183
And there are a
couple of places where

00:19:24.183 --> 00:19:26.740
I wanted to have very
long time outs on some

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:30.310
of our static pages and just
rely on invalidation when

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.710
they changed, and can't
do that, at least not yet.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:38.870
Another quote from my coworker
is he talks about magic tools.

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:40.790
And the one he
always brings up is

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:42.290
Splunk, about how
for certain things

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:43.924
it just makes life
so much easier.

00:19:43.924 --> 00:19:45.590
So I though I'd talk
a little about what

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:49.720
are these magic tools,
the things we just love?

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.060
So first off, BigQuery.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.260
I love BigQuery.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:55.874
We've talked about how
you use it for tests.

00:19:55.874 --> 00:19:57.790
We actually throw all
of our performance stats

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.636
into BigQuery.

00:19:59.636 --> 00:20:01.260
And what we do, like
an example of this

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:02.940
is if you're in the
browser, there's

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:05.440
this window.performance APIs
you can use to get things

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:07.582
about page load
times and how long

00:20:07.582 --> 00:20:09.790
it took you to download each
of the individual assets

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:10.324
in the page.

00:20:10.324 --> 00:20:11.740
It's all available
in the browser.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.535
And we actually send
those over the server,

00:20:13.535 --> 00:20:16.320
shove them into BigQuery.

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:17.700
And this is really useful.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.980
It's like, example where we
use, this was a while back.

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:22.444
And it was, I
think, after hours,

00:20:22.444 --> 00:20:24.360
and our users mostly use
our app 9:00 to 5:00.

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:27.180
And so you can see anomalies
easier at those times.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.150
And we saw this big spike in
page loads where they went--

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:33.480
usually, it's small
number of seconds or less.

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:35.966
And it went up to
close to minutes.

00:20:35.966 --> 00:20:36.840
I said, that's crazy.

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:38.010
So we dug into that.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.172
And it turns out, we
looked at our stats

00:20:40.172 --> 00:20:41.880
and, yes, we were
actually getting those.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:44.670
One user had a handful, I
think 100-plus page loads

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.190
that were taking over a minute,
when they should normally

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.830
take under a couple of seconds.

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:52.050
And then we did the join
against our back end data,

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:54.480
which we also have
dumped into BigQuery,

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.370
and the back end,
everything was fast.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.507
We started looking at
all the network downloads

00:20:58.507 --> 00:20:59.590
for the individual assets.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:01.412
All of those were fast.

00:21:01.412 --> 00:21:03.120
And at this point, we
started scrambling.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.120
We said, oh, what?

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.120
We realized, oh, there's
a new version of Chrome

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:06.911
that just came out.

00:21:06.911 --> 00:21:07.830
It must be that.

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:09.060
Turns out it kind of was.

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:13.510
Chrome had updated or slightly
modified how they throttled

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:14.569
tabs in the background.

00:21:14.569 --> 00:21:16.860
It turns out this user had
opened one of our list views

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.570
and on every single
entry in there,

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:20.549
had clicked Open in New Tab.

00:21:20.549 --> 00:21:22.590
And all those tabs opened,
ran long enough for us

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:24.960
to get the start of the
page load measurement,

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:26.290
and then got suspended.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:27.960
And then when the user finally
ended up clicking on one,

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.470
they'd finished the page load.

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:32.430
And since this user had
opened up hundreds of these,

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:34.800
by the time it got to the
end of them, many of those

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.157
had been sitting there
for quite a while.

00:21:37.157 --> 00:21:38.740
And so this was,
again, OK, it's easy.

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:42.840
We can get the time
backgrounded in our stats,

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.967
push that through, and just
ignore these in the future.

00:21:45.967 --> 00:21:47.550
Bigger thing here,
though, is actually

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:48.840
why is this user doing this?

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.162
And it turns out, well, we
should have bulk actions.

00:21:51.162 --> 00:21:53.120
And this was this, we
know, OK, let's implement

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:55.980
a bulk action for that.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:57.900
So we love BigQuery.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:01.600
Other things in here
for lessons learned

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:03.640
is we were initially using
a Stackdriver Logging

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:07.990
sync to get the stats from
our log files into BigQuery.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:10.760
And on our performance test
setups, that turned out to be--

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:13.052
we were actually losing a
fair number of logs that way.

00:22:13.052 --> 00:22:15.009
It was actually a fairly
high performance cost.

00:22:15.009 --> 00:22:17.740
So actually switched and started
calling the BigQuery APIs

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:18.670
directly.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:22.794
And since then, we've
had no problems.

00:22:22.794 --> 00:22:24.710
I think with BigQuery,
if you haven't used it,

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:27.000
it integrates really
nicely with Data Studio.

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.856
It makes it really easy
to spin up dashboards or--

00:22:29.856 --> 00:22:31.480
so we have that
happening all the time.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:33.320
A developer ships some
performance improvement,

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:35.778
they'll quickly spin up a Data
Studio dashboard that shows,

00:22:35.778 --> 00:22:39.740
look, things went like
that, or more normally, I

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:41.690
did this improvement
and nothing changed.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:44.131
But yeah, Data Studio.

00:22:44.131 --> 00:22:46.380
Something to keep an eye is
queries can get expensive.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.320
Were part of Google and
I tried to figure out

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:49.319
how much it was costing.

00:22:49.319 --> 00:22:51.320
And I couldn't actually
figure out how much

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.750
BigQuery was costing us.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:58.740
But yeah, so something
to keep an eye on.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:22:59.240
What else?

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:01.010
The trace.

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:02.615
This is also just very cool.

00:23:02.615 --> 00:23:06.164
It basically shows you
how long a request--

00:23:06.164 --> 00:23:07.580
each hop in the
request as it goes

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:10.344
through multiple app servers
or back ends, how long it took.

00:23:10.344 --> 00:23:12.260
You do have to integrate
with it a little bit.

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:13.760
But for a certain
class of problems,

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:17.396
it was just the
tool that worked.

00:23:17.396 --> 00:23:18.520
Let's see, a Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:19.686
Another thing we just loved.

00:23:19.686 --> 00:23:23.010
We were a client of this for
some of the public Gmail APIs

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:26.520
where we get a message for
each new email coming in.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.530
We also use this
internally for sending data

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:31.770
to our indexers for a
full-text search, basically.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:33.550
And the failure modes
for this are great.

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:35.944
It's easy to use.

00:23:35.944 --> 00:23:37.610
Yeah, it's something
we should use more.

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:44.237
If you've used our app, we
look like a first-party app

00:23:44.237 --> 00:23:45.820
from Google, kind
of indistinguishable

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:46.944
from the other Google apps.

00:23:46.944 --> 00:23:48.910
But we're actually
built using, basically,

00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:52.350
all of the public APIs that
any third parties can use.

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:54.610
And so what do we use?

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:56.440
So I talked a bit
about BigQuery,

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:59.170
talked about Cloud
Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage.

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:00.790
We use the Key
Management Service.

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:03.310
This basically lets us get
crypto keys out of our source

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:03.940
code.

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:06.610
I mentioned you
should be checking

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.861
all of your configuration
into your source repository.

00:24:08.861 --> 00:24:11.276
And if you do that, what do
you do about your crypto keys?

00:24:11.276 --> 00:24:12.490
You don't want them there.

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:13.990
You can go search
on GitHub and find

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.784
all these people have
published their crypto keys

00:24:16.784 --> 00:24:17.950
for the entire world to see.

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:19.491
You don't want that
to happen to you.

00:24:19.491 --> 00:24:22.600
So Key Management Service
lets you avoid that.

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:25.810
The other big one we use here
is Cloud Talent Solutions.

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.760
I believe we have a
talk here on Thursday,

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:29.980
I think it's MLAI 108.

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:32.399
Should definitely
go to that talk.

00:24:32.399 --> 00:24:34.940
But what this is, we use this
for all of our in-app candidate

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:36.012
searching.

00:24:36.012 --> 00:24:37.720
And we went through
a bunch of iterations

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:42.070
here, from just doing straight
up Spanner queries for search

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:42.640
in our app.

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:45.192
Then we switched to using
Solr for free text search.

00:24:45.192 --> 00:24:47.650
And we actually switched to
another kind of internal Google

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:48.774
thing for free text search.

00:24:48.774 --> 00:24:50.860
And then at that point
realized, actually search

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:52.390
isn't our problem.

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:53.680
It's not just matching.

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:56.200
If you're trying to search
for candidates, jobs, skills,

00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:58.540
those kind of things, you
need something smarter.

00:24:58.540 --> 00:24:59.592
And that's what this is.

00:24:59.592 --> 00:25:01.300
The example they would
bring is if you're

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:04.420
searching for an executive
or an executive assistant,

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:05.641
those are very different.

00:25:05.641 --> 00:25:07.390
And you don't really
want searches for one

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:08.210
to show the other.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.454
But if you're doing naive
free text search, they do.

00:25:10.454 --> 00:25:11.870
And so this Cloud
Talent Solutions

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:14.291
solves all those problems for
you across all industries.

00:25:14.291 --> 00:25:16.540
And it does things like I
think it's even smart enough

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:20.580
to know that if you type
in military job titles,

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:24.100
it knows how to map those
to civilian job titles.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:30.352
And I think it's job
titles, locations, skills,

00:25:30.352 --> 00:25:31.560
and a number of other things.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:32.768
So definitely check that out.

00:25:32.768 --> 00:25:33.730
Check out that talk.

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:35.354
How do we actually
integrate with this?

00:25:35.354 --> 00:25:37.570
Our app just, in-line,
makes direct API calls out

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:39.230
to Cloud Talent Solutions.

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:41.900
We're actually using Cloud
Pub/Sub to send whenever

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:46.210
we get a new candidate, new
resume uploaded in our app,

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:49.030
we push it into a Cloud Pub/Sub,
have separate workers that

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.320
pop it out, and then make
the API calls to add those

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:53.154
to Cloud Talent Solutions.

00:25:53.154 --> 00:25:54.570
Another place we're
using Pub/Sub.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:57.130
Really like it.

00:25:57.130 --> 00:25:58.990
Let's see, what else?

00:25:58.990 --> 00:25:59.770
We use Sheets.

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:01.900
Quote from the tech
lead of the Sheets team,

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:03.010
I was talking to him
a little while ago,

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.384
was that Sheets is the second
best tool in the enterprise

00:26:05.384 --> 00:26:06.340
for doing anything.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:07.640
And our users love that.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:08.890
They really love Sheets.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.350
And so we've been focusing
on doing basically full data

00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:15.580
exports to Sheets, as well as
for some of our in-app reports,

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:19.000
basically a cooked version of
that report data to Sheets.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.820
And this lets our users
modify the report,

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:24.250
build their own reports
and do some modification.

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:26.937
We get lots of requests for--

00:26:26.937 --> 00:26:29.020
we like this report, but
I need to actually change

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:29.610
a little bit.

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:31.270
Oh, that person wasn't
actually hired by me.

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:32.990
He was hired by the other
person, even though in the--

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:33.516
Yeah.

00:26:33.516 --> 00:26:34.390
All sorts of excuses.

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:36.850
They want to be able to edit
the reports before they actually

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:37.933
show them to other people.

00:26:37.933 --> 00:26:41.570
And we didn't want to build that
in so we put that into Sheets.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:43.510
Drive, we use that for
basically any place

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:46.420
you upload a file in our app.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.760
Gmail, we're doing
two things there.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:51.040
We actually have a
Gmail add on, which

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:54.160
basically lets you see hiring
data directly in Gmail.

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:56.320
So you can seamlessly,
if you're corresponding

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:58.970
with a candidate via email, you
can see what we know about it

00:26:58.970 --> 00:26:59.950
and hire there.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.640
Anyone can write one of those.

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:05.980
We're also using the
Gmail APIs basically

00:27:05.980 --> 00:27:08.110
ingest email conversations
between people

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:09.651
on the hiring team
and the candidates

00:27:09.651 --> 00:27:11.677
so you can view it in our app.

00:27:11.677 --> 00:27:14.260
Some lessons learned on Gmail,
Gmail has a billion users, lots

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:15.787
of servers, and
it's totally willing

00:27:15.787 --> 00:27:17.620
to do a denial of service
attack against you

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.380
if you're not ready for it.

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:22.330
Also just some of the
data shape can be weird.

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:25.312
We kind of expected,
oh, any given candidate

00:27:25.312 --> 00:27:27.520
will have a few hundred
messages associated with them

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:29.650
between the hiring team and--

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.050
you can get it to the point
where especially some people

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:33.340
have hundreds of thousands.

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:36.380
And if you're not ready for
that, that can be a problem.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:40.355
Calendar, done a lot
with calendar there.

00:27:40.355 --> 00:27:42.730
We've actually built what we
call a multi-slot scheduler.

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:44.104
If you look at
the interview day,

00:27:44.104 --> 00:27:48.760
it's one person meeting a bunch
of people in a bunch of rooms.

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:50.920
And so we've built this
multi-slot scheduler.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:52.370
It's actually
bidirectional sync.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:55.560
So in Calendar, you can
adjust an interview schedule

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:57.599
and it will reflect on
our app, and vice versa.

00:27:57.599 --> 00:27:59.515
And all of this is built
with the public APIs.

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:05.369
And here's a quote from one
of the engineers on this.

00:28:05.369 --> 00:28:05.910
"Don't do it.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:06.993
Calendars are really hard.

00:28:06.993 --> 00:28:08.730
All sorts of crazy
corner cases."

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:10.926
That's true, but it's by
far our most loved feature.

00:28:10.926 --> 00:28:12.300
So definitely do
it, just realize

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:13.920
what you're signing up for.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:16.569
These APIs are powerful,
and calendars--

00:28:16.569 --> 00:28:17.860
you might think they're simple.

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:18.600
They're not.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:22.080
You have to deal with non-Google
calendars, time zones,

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:25.545
weird configuration settings,
permissions, spam filters.

00:28:27.975 --> 00:28:29.850
Or more if you're doing
stuff with Calendars,

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.479
just realize that the
APIs are really powerful.

00:28:32.479 --> 00:28:34.020
And what you're
going to get is going

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.811
to look so seamless
that your customers are

00:28:35.811 --> 00:28:38.040
going to want you to provide
support for Calendar.

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.300
And we're part of Google.

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:40.716
And where we
actually have trouble

00:28:40.716 --> 00:28:43.504
is that with the internal
access guidelines,

00:28:43.504 --> 00:28:45.420
there's no way I can
help one of our customers

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:46.461
debug a calendar problem.

00:28:46.461 --> 00:28:47.710
That's some other team.

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:49.335
And if you're outside
of Google, that's

00:28:49.335 --> 00:28:50.980
going to be a problem, as well.

00:28:50.980 --> 00:28:52.670
And one last lesson
learned here is

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.989
that once you start a calendar,
your tests have time in them.

00:28:55.989 --> 00:28:57.530
And once you have
time in your tests,

00:28:57.530 --> 00:29:00.650
your tests are going to fail
on New Year's Day, New Year's

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:05.240
Eve, leap years, leap days,
daylight savings time.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:06.334
I've lived with it.

00:29:06.334 --> 00:29:07.250
It's kind of annoying.

00:29:12.691 --> 00:29:13.190
Let's see.

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:16.800
Lessons learned In the
past several years,

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.580
I've learned way more lessons
than I can keep track of.

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:20.940
Here's the top couple.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:22.410
And some of them are going
to seem really obvious,

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.076
but since they're
things I had to learn,

00:29:24.076 --> 00:29:26.230
I might as well talk about them.

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:27.910
So this is automate everything.

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:29.950
People screw up.

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.060
And at least if your
software makes a mistake,

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:35.274
you can write a test, fix the
bug, file the bug, fix it.

00:29:35.274 --> 00:29:36.190
It won't happen again.

00:29:36.190 --> 00:29:39.250
You can't really
do that with users.

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:41.470
And so I'm claiming that we
built a wildly successful

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:42.820
application with happy users.

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:44.710
And ways we've
actually screwed up

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:47.680
is we've deployed ancient builds
to production, as in months

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:48.220
old.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:53.020
We've deployed builds we hadn't
qualified yet to production.

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:55.620
I would say we've done most
of these before we did that.

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:57.370
In my journey of
those four slides

00:29:57.370 --> 00:30:00.872
is the most of those were prior
to our end point we are now.

00:30:00.872 --> 00:30:02.970
We've actually destroyed
our entire staging system

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.530
more times than I can imagine.

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:06.760
This is the case, luckily,
is all of our servers

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:08.885
are stateless, all of the
configuration checked in.

00:30:08.885 --> 00:30:11.080
It's really easy just
to deploy it again.

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:15.070
We've actually destroyed all
of our production app servers.

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:17.280
We almost use this as a
troubleshooting problem.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.420
If something weird is going on,
just kill all the app servers.

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:23.230
Like the whole turn it off
and turn it on again thing.

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:23.770
Still works.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:28.840
I think this was before
we had paying customers.

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:31.215
We actually did destroy all
of our production system once

00:30:31.215 --> 00:30:32.815
and had to restore from backup.

00:30:35.244 --> 00:30:36.660
The other thing
here is at Google,

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:39.489
we practice what we call
blameless post-mortem culture.

00:30:39.489 --> 00:30:41.030
This is where, when
someone screws up

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:43.230
is you don't say, hey,
you're fired, get outta here.

00:30:43.230 --> 00:30:45.646
You say, OK, you need to write
up a post-mortem about what

00:30:45.646 --> 00:30:46.440
went wrong.

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:48.570
Talk about what went
wrong, where you got lucky,

00:30:48.570 --> 00:30:50.985
where you got unlucky, and
list a set of action items

00:30:50.985 --> 00:30:53.500
to prevent this from
ever happening again.

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:55.321
And usually those are automate.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.002
Another quote from
my colleague Bo.

00:31:01.002 --> 00:31:02.210
"Test that your backups work.

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:03.043
Everyone knows this.

00:31:03.043 --> 00:31:05.250
Only 10% of teams do this."

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.500
And yeah, everyone knows this.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:08.730
I would say test that
your restores work.

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:11.056
Who cares if the
backups are going OK?

00:31:11.056 --> 00:31:15.680
And I did say we have actually
had to restore from backup,

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:17.030
and it might have been me.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:18.350
I'm sorry.

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:21.950
But luckily you can always keep
your existing database around

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.440
and figure out what happened
during that interval

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:28.642
and replay it before customers
show up the next morning.

00:31:28.642 --> 00:31:30.100
Yeah, definitely
test your backups.

00:31:33.411 --> 00:31:34.360
Let's see.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:35.409
I say lame duck mode.

00:31:35.409 --> 00:31:36.700
That's kind of an awesome duck.

00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:38.616
But what this is is it
talks about when you're

00:31:38.616 --> 00:31:40.810
doing either a rolling
update or scaling up,

00:31:40.810 --> 00:31:43.795
scaling down is how you deal
with requests to your existing

00:31:43.795 --> 00:31:46.907
app servers, or the app servers
that are being torn down.

00:31:46.907 --> 00:31:48.740
And it's one of those
things, those, oh yes,

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:49.240
we should do this.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:50.269
We'll get around to it.

00:31:50.269 --> 00:31:51.310
It's one of those things.

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:52.590
But it's a big deal.

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:55.260
And so we ignored it
for a really long time.

00:31:55.260 --> 00:31:57.400
And then what happened
is somewhat embarrassing

00:31:57.400 --> 00:31:59.220
is our sales team came
to us and is saying,

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:00.303
we're running these demos.

00:32:00.303 --> 00:32:01.870
And every once in
a while, things

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:03.610
slow down, they get really slow.

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:05.290
And it gets awkward
because they run out

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:06.445
of things to talk about.

00:32:06.445 --> 00:32:08.320
And if your sales team
is saying that they're

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:09.820
running out of
things to talk about,

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:12.364
that's definitely a problem.

00:32:12.364 --> 00:32:13.780
So what we did was
we had actually

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.270
been tracking what
our P99 page loads.

00:32:16.270 --> 00:32:18.659
And all those looked good.

00:32:18.659 --> 00:32:20.950
And so then we dug some more
and said, OK, actually no,

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:22.449
the sales people
aren't lying to us.

00:32:22.449 --> 00:32:24.210
Things are actually
slow sometimes.

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:25.810
And a big portion
of this was actually

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:27.490
from how we were dealing
with these rolling updates.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.215
And example of what
would happen is

00:32:29.215 --> 00:32:30.880
if we were either
a new app server

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:34.017
comes alive, either from a
rolling update or auto scaling,

00:32:34.017 --> 00:32:36.100
we would start-- we were
actually sending requests

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:38.987
to it before it was
ready to handle those.

00:32:38.987 --> 00:32:40.945
And then either they
would fail and get bounced

00:32:40.945 --> 00:32:42.430
to another app
server, or worse, they

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:44.971
would hang out there and wait
for the database initialization

00:32:44.971 --> 00:32:46.240
of other stuff to finish.

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:47.650
And you get a slow request.

00:32:47.650 --> 00:32:49.540
Again, this is kind of
super obvious things.

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:50.998
And there's great
support for this.

00:32:50.998 --> 00:32:52.120
We just weren't doing it.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:54.487
Similar one was, as we were
tearing down app servers,

00:32:54.487 --> 00:32:57.070
you'd get the case of a somewhat
longer request would come in.

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:58.580
One app server would
get partway done.

00:32:58.580 --> 00:32:59.680
App server would get killed.

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:01.096
It would get sent
to the next one.

00:33:01.096 --> 00:33:02.500
Since we were
rolling updates, it

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:05.170
could sometimes bounce
between multiple app server.

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:09.910
Kind of embarrassing.

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:12.550
And so the things here again,
it's all in the documentation.

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:13.060
Do it.

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:15.850
If you're using Kubernetes,
you have a readiness check.

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.080
Make sure your readiness
check is actually correct.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:19.496
I think our first
version of that,

00:33:19.496 --> 00:33:21.239
we were reporting
readiness when we

00:33:21.239 --> 00:33:23.530
thought we were ready, not
when we were actually ready.

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:26.230
There's liveness checks
you need you can implement.

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:28.269
And what else do I have here?

00:33:28.269 --> 00:33:30.310
Oh, I guess also if you're
looking at Kubernetes,

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:32.530
make sure you have
a sick term handler.

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.840
And then also look at
your configure connection

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:35.925
draining timeouts.

00:33:35.925 --> 00:33:38.830
Those also matter here.

00:33:38.830 --> 00:33:40.230
And this was something--

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:42.240
actually, another place
where Cloud Trace really

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:44.930
helped us is looking at these
individually really slow

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:45.430
requests.

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:49.920
All right.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.770
Last but not least is have
an awesome design and product

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:53.910
team.

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:55.832
I probably should
have said this first.

00:33:55.832 --> 00:33:57.540
It doesn't matter how
technically awesome

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:00.390
your application is if ordinary
humans either can't figure out

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:04.047
how to use it or
don't want to use it.

00:34:04.047 --> 00:34:05.880
This is something that
Cloud Platform is not

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:07.630
going to help you
with right now,

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:11.340
at least until the ML and AI
teams get their act together.

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:15.194
So you really need good
product and design people.

00:34:15.194 --> 00:34:17.610
And I know this personally as
our first couple of versions

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:18.810
of Hire, we actually
didn't really

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:20.110
have a design team at the time.

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:22.110
And they were pretty
embarrassingly bad.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:23.969
They were designed
by people like me.

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:26.969
And nothing worse than realizing
that the software you designed

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:27.877
is awful.

00:34:27.877 --> 00:34:29.460
So don't let that
happen to your team.

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:31.620
Love your designers.

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.120
And they totally didn't pay
me to put this slide up here.

00:34:37.469 --> 00:34:38.010
All right.

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:40.409
That's basically it.

00:34:40.409 --> 00:34:42.659
If your team is hiring and
need help with the process,

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.239
go to hire.google.com
to check us out.

00:34:45.239 --> 00:34:46.920
If you're looking
for a job, we're

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:49.500
looking for a back end, front
end, designers, product people.

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:50.910
Come talk to me afterwards.

00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:54.860
[THEME MUSIC]

